# modules/iac/cloudformation.rules - CloudFormation checks via the pattern-rule
# engine (docs/DESIGN.md §13 step 4's remaining cloud-half item; §8.2's own
# catalog line names this file directly: "cloud.rules terraform.rules
# cloudformation.rules").
#
# Path note: same frozen path table row as terraform.rules, helm.rules and
# kubernetes.rules (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's `modules/iac/*.rules` row) -
# flat, directly under modules/iac/, never nested, never named
# `checks.rules` (reserved repository-wide for the §9.5 script-check schema
# and matched first in records_schema_for_path).
#
# Seed rules, straight out of docs/DESIGN.md §8.2's own list, translated from
# Terraform's HCL attribute shape into CloudFormation's YAML/JSON property
# shape: open ingress CIDR, disabled/public S3 access blocking, disabled
# encryption at rest, missing KMS key rotation, AssociatePublicIpAddress,
# hardcoded secrets, and a publicly accessible RDS instance - plus one check
# with no Terraform sibling, the privileged ECS container
# (IAC-CFN-ECS_PRIVILEGED-01), which is the CloudFormation spelling of
# kubernetes.rules' own IAC-K8S-PRIVILEGED-01.
#
# THE SCOPING PROBLEM THIS PACK EXISTS TO SOLVE, stated up front because it
# shapes every rule below: unlike Terraform (`*.tf`, a format no other tool
# in this repo emits) and unlike Helm (`values.yaml` / `templates/*.yaml`,
# a path convention scoursh can lean on), a genuine CloudFormation template
# has NO reserved filename or path convention at all - docs/DESIGN.md §8.2
# itself says the walk is over `*.yaml`/`*.yml`/`*.json` generically.  A bare
# Kubernetes manifest, a Helm chart source, a docker-compose file, or any
# other unrelated YAML/JSON in the same repository is exactly as likely to
# carry that same extension.  `files:` globs alone therefore CANNOT do the
# disambiguation terraform.rules and helm.rules both rely on; every rule
# below is deliberately broad on `files:` (`*.yaml`, `*.yml`, `*.json`,
# `*.template` - every extension a real CloudFormation template is shipped
# under) and instead does the real narrowing with a `context-require` that a
# genuine `Type: AWS::<Service>::<Resource>` (or its quoted JSON spelling,
# `"Type": "AWS::<Service>::<Resource>"`) declaration sits within the
# window: that string is CloudFormation's own resource-type discriminator,
# is never emitted by a Kubernetes manifest (which declares
# `apiVersion`/`kind`, not `Type: AWS::...`), a Helm chart (same Kubernetes
# vocabulary, plus Go-template directives), or a docker-compose file
# (`services:`/`image:`, no `Type:` key at all) - so it is the one anchor
# available that is genuinely CloudFormation-specific regardless of what the
# surrounding file happens to be named.  IAC-CFN-KEY_ROTATION_DISABLED-01
# needs no `context-require` for this because its own `pattern` IS that
# anchor.
#
# The globs are written `*.yaml`, NOT `**/*.yaml`.  Per §9.1.2 an unanchored
# glob "may match at any depth" - sast_glob_match tries it against every
# path-segment-aligned suffix - so `*.yaml` already covers `infra/main.yaml`.
# `**/*.yaml` translates to `^(.*/)?.*/[^/]*\.yaml$`, whose `.*/` requires at
# least one directory component, so it would silently MISS a template sitting
# at the scan root (`main.yaml` in a repo whose toplevel is the scan root) -
# the single most likely place for one.  Every sibling pack in this directory
# uses the bare form for the same reason.
#
# WHY THERE IS NO `exclude-files` HERE, unlike kubernetes.rules.  That pack
# carries eight `exclude-files` compose globs because its patterns key on
# `image:`/`privileged:`/`resources:` - vocabulary a docker-compose file uses
# verbatim - so nothing in the pattern itself could tell the two shapes
# apart, and it cross-fired on `docker-compose.*.yml` from its first commit.
# This pack has the opposite property: the `Type: AWS::...` anchor is a
# STRICTLY STRONGER discriminator than any filename glob, because it is
# content a docker-compose file (or a Kubernetes manifest, or a Helm chart)
# cannot contain while still being that thing.  Adding compose globs here
# would be a guard no fixture can distinguish from its absence - i.e. an
# untestable line - so the boundary is asserted the other way instead:
# tests/suites/iac.sh scans tests/fixtures/iac/docker-compose/ and
# tests/fixtures/iac/helm/ with this pack and requires zero IAC-CFN-*
# findings.  If that assertion ever goes red, `exclude-files` is the fix; it
# is not the prophylactic.
#
# Engine note: exactly like helm.rules and kubernetes.rules, neither this
# engine nor any other module parses YAML or JSON - `docs/FOUNDATION.md`
# tension 25 makes the same point for SCA's lockfile formats - so both a
# CloudFormation YAML template and its JSON twin are scanned as
# line-oriented text via `scan_match`/`scan_match_offsets`
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10.3), never a real parse tree.  Each pattern below
# is written to match either spelling: an unquoted YAML key
# (`CidrIp: 0.0.0.0/0`) or a quoted JSON key/value pair
# (`"CidrIp": "0.0.0.0/0"`), via an optional leading/trailing `"` on the key
# and an optional `"` around the value.  A minified single-line JSON
# document is the same known, documented window gap kubernetes.rules already
# records for itself.
#
# IAC-CFN-KEY_ROTATION_DISABLED-01 is this pack's one ABSENCE check, the same
# shape as terraform.rules' own IAC-TF-KEY_ROTATION_DISABLED-01 and
# java.rules' SAST-JAVA-XXE_DISALLOW_DOCTYPE-01 before it: the finding fires
# on a `Type: AWS::KMS::Key` resource declaration unless `EnableKeyRotation:
# true` appears within the context-window.  An absence check can only be a
# `context-deny` with a non-zero window (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10.2 -
# context-window:0 binds same-line-intent guards only), so it carries the
# same reviewed W033 trade-off terraform.rules' own copy of this check does.
#
# IAC-CFN-HARDCODED_SECRET-01 shares its `context-window` between a
# `context-require` (the `Type: AWS::` anchor above) and a `context-deny`
# (the placeholder-literal allowlist terraform.rules/helm.rules both use at
# `context-window: 0`) - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.1 fixes `context-window` as
# ONE value per rule, shared by every context-require/context-deny entry, so
# the two cannot be given independent windows here.  Widening the deny's
# window past 0 is the "not the safe direction" trade-off
# `docs/FOUNDATION.md` tension 3/§10.2 names (a placeholder token elsewhere
# in the same resource block could mask a genuine adjacent secret literal),
# accepted deliberately here - the same way terraform.rules' own
# IAC-TF-KEY_ROTATION_DISABLED-01 accepts W033 - because without it there is
# no way to require the CloudFormation-specific anchor at all: the other
# checks in this pack that also share a window do not carry a context-deny,
# so they keep the require-only, no-trade-off shape.

id: IAC-CFN-OPEN_CIDR-01
title: CloudFormation security group ingress open to 0.0.0.0/0 or ::/0
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A02:2025
pattern: "?Cidr(Ip|Ipv6)"?\s*:\s*"?(0\.0\.0\.0/0|::/0)"?
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-require: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::EC2::SecurityGroup(Ingress)?"?
context-window: 15
tags: static
tags: compliance
cis: 5.2
cis: 5.3
remediation: Restrict CidrIp/CidrIpv6 to the smallest network that needs access. Where
  public reachability is genuinely required (a public load balancer listener), terminate
  it at an edge that applies WAF and rate limiting rather than at the instance security
  group.
references: CIS-AWS-5.2

id: IAC-CFN-S3_PUBLIC_ACCESS-01
title: S3 bucket disables a public-access-block control, or sets a public canned ACL
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A02:2025
pattern: "?(BlockPublicAcls|BlockPublicPolicy|IgnorePublicAcls|RestrictPublicBuckets)"?\s*:\s*"?[Ff]alse"?|"?AccessControl"?\s*:\s*"?[Pp]ublic-?[Rr]ead(-?[Ww]rite)?"?
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-require: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::S3::Bucket"?
context-window: 15
tags: static
tags: compliance
cis: 2.1.1
remediation: Set every PublicAccessBlockConfiguration flag (BlockPublicAcls,
  BlockPublicPolicy, IgnorePublicAcls, RestrictPublicBuckets) to true and remove any
  public canned ACL. If the object truly must be public, front it with CloudFront and
  an Origin Access Control instead of a public bucket.
references: CWE-284
references: CIS-AWS-2.1.1

id: IAC-CFN-UNENCRYPTED-01
title: RDS or EBS resource explicitly configured without encryption at rest
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A04:2025
pattern: "?StorageEncrypted"?\s*:\s*"?[Ff]alse"?
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-require: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::(RDS::DB(Instance|Cluster)|EC2::Volume)"?
context-window: 15
tags: static
tags: compliance
cis: 2.1.1
remediation: Set StorageEncrypted to true and, where the resource supports it, pin a
  customer-managed KMS key via KmsKeyId rather than relying on the account default key.
references: CWE-311

id: IAC-CFN-KEY_ROTATION_DISABLED-01
title: KMS key resource declared without automatic key rotation enabled
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-320
owasp: A04:2025
pattern: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::KMS::Key"?
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-deny: "?EnableKeyRotation"?\s*:\s*"?[Tt]rue"?
context-window: 12
tags: static
remediation: Add EnableKeyRotation: true to the AWS::KMS::Key resource so AWS rotates
  the backing key material annually without any application-visible change (KMS
  transparently keeps prior key material available for decrypting old ciphertext).
references: CWE-320
references: CIS-AWS-3.8
cis: 3.8

id: IAC-CFN-PUBLIC_IP-01
title: EC2 instance or launch template auto-assigns a public IP address
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A02:2025
pattern: "?AssociatePublicIpAddress"?\s*:\s*"?[Tt]rue"?
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-require: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::EC2::(Instance|LaunchTemplate)"?
context-window: 15
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Set AssociatePublicIpAddress to false and reach the instance through a
  bastion, a VPN, or a load balancer in a public subnet instead of giving the instance
  itself a public address. If a public address is genuinely required, pair it with a
  security group scoped to specific source ranges, not 0.0.0.0/0.
references: CWE-284

id: IAC-CFN-HARDCODED_SECRET-01
title: Hardcoded credential literal in a CloudFormation resource property
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2025
pattern: \b(Password|Secret|SecretKey|ApiKey|AccessKey|Token|MasterUserPassword|ClientSecret)\b"?\s*:\s*"?[^"'!{\s][^"'\n]{7,}
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-require: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::
context-deny: (CHANGEME|changeme|EXAMPLE|example|XXXXXXXX|xxxxxxxx|placeholder|dummy|TODO)
context-window: 10
tags: static
remediation: Remove the literal credential from the template and rotate it immediately;
  a committed secret must be treated as compromised even if the commit is later reverted,
  because it remains in git history. Source the value from an existing Secrets
  Manager/SSM SecureString resource via a dynamic reference
  ('{{resolve:secretsmanager:...}}' / '{{resolve:ssm-secure:...}}') instead of a literal.
references: CWE-798

id: IAC-CFN-RDS_PUBLIC-01
title: RDS instance configured as publicly accessible
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A02:2025
pattern: "?PubliclyAccessible"?\s*:\s*"?[Tt]rue"?
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-require: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::RDS::DBInstance"?
context-window: 15
tags: static
tags: compliance
cis: 2.3.2
remediation: Set PubliclyAccessible to false and reach the database through an
  application tier inside the same VPC, a bastion, or a VPN, never a public endpoint.
references: CWE-284
references: CIS-AWS-2.3.2

id: IAC-CFN-ECS_PRIVILEGED-01
title: ECS task definition runs a container with privileged escalation enabled
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A02:2025
pattern: "?Privileged"?\s*:\s*"?[Tt]rue"?
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
files: *.template
context-require: "?Type"?\s*:\s*"?AWS::ECS::TaskDefinition"?
context-window: 15
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Remove the Privileged property from the container definition (it defaults to
  false). A privileged ECS container gets near-complete access to the container instance -
  device nodes, kernel capabilities, and namespaces - so almost every real task can run
  instead with a narrow, explicit linuxParameters.capabilities.add list for only the
  capability it actually needs. On Fargate the property is not supported at all.
references: CWE-269
